\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.9}{\ignorespaces \textbf {NFW enclosed mass and shell mass fraction.} Left axis: monotonically increasing enclosed mass $M(<r)$ with radius. Right axis: shell mass fraction per logarithmic shell. The outer halo contains a substantial fraction of the total mass budget, indicating that it cannot be ignored when computing potential depths.}}{34}{figure.caption.17}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.10}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Shell contributions to the central potential depth.} Individual shells (blue points with error bars) contribute to the total potential depth $|\Phi (0)|$. The cumulative contribution (orange line) from all shells beyond a given radius shows that shells at radii beyond the scale radius $r_s$ still make substantial contributions to the escape-speed scale. This supports the argument that the outer halo cannot be treated as a boundary condition; its potential structure must be represented accurately.}}{35}{figure.caption.18}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.11}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Outer-potential fraction versus cutoff radius.} For probe radii ranging from the center to $r = 5 r_s$, the fraction of the potential originating from material beyond the cutoff radius $r_{\mathrm {cut}}$ is plotted against the cutoff. As the cutoff moves outward, the outer contribution drops sharply initially, then plateaus. For the central region ($r_{\mathrm {probe}} < r_s$), a cutoff at $r_{\mathrm {cut}} \approx 10$ kpc retains roughly 5--10\% of the potential from the outer halo; at $r_{\mathrm {cut}} \approx 30$ kpc, this drops below 1\%. This diagnostic directly guides the choice of truncation radius: if the outer halo contributes significantly to the potential at the radii of interest (e.g., $r < 1$ kpc for the central density), then the outer region must be represented accurately.}}{36}{figure.caption.19}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.12}{\ignorespaces \textbf {2D map of the outer-potential fraction.} Rows represent probe radii; columns represent cutoff radii. The color intensity indicates the fraction of potential at a given probe radius arising from material beyond the cutoff. The transition from high (red) to low (blue) values shows the radius beyond which the outer halo becomes a small correction. This visualization is useful for selecting practical values of $r_{\mathrm {cut}}$ for different regions of interest.}}{37}{figure.caption.20}%
